── 3c. against the REAL workflow tree — every required context still resolves to a job that publishes it ──
  ok   the probe feed IS the committed required set — 4 name(s) read out of .github/required-checks.json, never typed here, so the fixture cannot be written so as to miss the defect
  ok   the real-tree run EXITS 0, and that status is asserted separately from the count — a refusal leaves REAL_UNMAPPED at 0, so a count-only clause reads clean on a generator that died
  ok   REAL_UNMAPPED is 0 and it is READ: all 4 committed required context(s) resolve to a NAMED job in .github/workflows/ — Cloud gate <- cloud.yml job 'cloud-gate'; Console gate <- console-harness.yml job 'console-gate'; Elixir gate <- elixir.yml job 'elixir-gate'; PR references an active task <- pr-task-gate.yml job 'pr-task-gate'; 
  ok   the mutation APPLIES: EXACTLY ONE `name: Cloud gate` line — cloud.yml job 'cloud-gate' — is rewritten in a COPY of the real tree, so the twin below is not asserting over an unchanged file
  ok   …and renaming that one job in the COPY takes REAL_UNMAPPED from 0 to 1 and names 'Cloud gate' — the clean verdict above is a READ of the workflow tree, not a silence (main's clause could not move at all: it asserted on the static spec file)
  ok   …and the text it reds WITH names the workflow (cloud.yml), the job ('cloud-gate') and the required context it feeds ('Cloud gate') — the operator is told what to fix, not merely that something is wrong
  ok   …and a catch-all planted in a COPY of the real tree REFUSES (exit 1) while REAL_UNMAPPED stays 0 — the exit-code arm is load-bearing, and a clause that asserted only the count would have called this clean

